Setting. Grothendieck uses a half-edge ('arc') formalism for graphs. A graph G is a set of vertices S, a set of arcs Ã with a fixed-point-free involution σ_Ã, and an incidence map σ_G: Ã → S. The edges are A = Ã/σ_Ã. A 'generalized morphism' f: G' → G sends vertices to vertices. Each arc goes either to an arc or to an 'identity', i.e. it is collapsed to a vertex. This is the category of graphs with edge contractions that indexes the strata at infinity of M̄_{g,ν}.

Previous page. A 'morphisme de générisation' satisfies these conditions:
- S' → S is surjective;
- the fibres G'(s) are connected;
- the arcs not collapsed, B̃' ⊂ Ã', map bijectively onto Ã: (50) Ã ≅ B̃', compatibly with the involutions.

Let G'_• be the subgraph with all vertices S' and the collapsed edges A' \ B'. Then (49) S ≅ π_0(G'_•).

This page.
- (51) The incidence map of G is recovered as the composite Ã ≅ B̃' → S' → π_0(G'_•) ≅ S. Hence G and f are determined up to isomorphism by the pair (G', B').
- (52) G is a pushout ('somme amalgamée', cocartesian square) in the category of graphs with generalized morphisms:
  - G = G' ⊔_{G'_•} π_0(G'_•);
  - π_0(G'_•) is viewed as a discrete graph (no edges; 'discret' replaces the struck-out 'ponctuel');
  - G'_• → G' is the canonical inclusion;
  - G'_• → π_0(G'_•) is the canonical projection, which is a generalized morphism because it collapses every edge.
- Equivalently, G' → G is universal among generalized morphisms out of G' that send the arcs of Ã' \ B̃' to identities.

Next page. With C' = A' \ B', he writes G = G'/C' ('contracting the edges of C' to points'). He matches this with the topological intuition of maps of geometric realizations that collapse edges. He notes the pushout exists for any edge set C' ⊂ A'. He then begins proving that every generalized morphism factors uniquely as a contraction followed by something else, presumably an ordinary morphism.

Purpose. This sets up the combinatorics of dual graphs of stable curves and their specializations ('générisation'), i.e. the poset/category of boundary strata of M̄_{g,ν} for the structure at infinity.
